Patch 26: skip the dry-pass trellis only on transform blocks where it is
expensive. Supersedes patch 0025 -- apply INSTEAD of it.

Standalone, against anchor 5d628d8.

WHAT 0025 MEASURED. Skipping the trellis in tx_type_rd for every dry-pass block
gave, at Speed 3:

    A1  +3.26% / +0.14% BD   ratio 23.3   (bar 25, miss by 1.7)
    A2  +4.23% / +0.17% BD   ratio 24.9   (bar 25, miss by 0.1)

and at Speed 4, A1 +1.23% / +0.26% (ratio 4.7) and A2 +3.86% / +0.20% (19.3).
Speed 3 is the preset worth pursuing; Speed 4 should be abandoned for this
change, where it buys little and costs more.

The Speed-3 gap is the smallest this project has faced. A1 needs its BD-rate to
fall 7% at constant speed. A2 needs 0.4%.

WHY A SIZE SPLIT RATHER THAN A THRESHOLD. Patch 0021 established the method:
when a bundled change fails on ratio, split it and keep the members that are
cheap in quality. Disabling only the uneven 4-way partitions kept 39% of the
family's speedup for 6% of its BD-rate cost -- ratio 207.7, where the whole
family gave 30.6. Every threshold move on this project has slid along the
trade-off instead; that split moved off it.

The equivalent split here is transform block size. Trellis cost scales with the
number of coefficients, so large blocks carry most of the time. Ranking damage
does not scale the same way: on a small block each coefficient is a larger
share of the block's rate, so replacing the trellis with the cheap quantiser
shifts the RD estimate proportionally more. Large blocks are where skipping is
expensive in time and cheap in quality -- the members worth keeping.

So the trellis is skipped only when the transform block's short side is at
least AVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM; below that the dry pass keeps it.

MEASURED LOCALLY (192x128, 4 frames, cpu-used=3, paired against the same
baseline binary), speedup relative to unpatched:

    0025, skip everywhere              +8.00%
    threshold  8, keep 4x4 only        +7.26%   (91% of 0025's speedup)
    threshold 16, keep 4x4 and 8x8     +4.19%   (52% of 0025's speedup)

All three produce distinct bitstreams. Local runs cannot price BD-rate, so what
the numbers establish is the speed side of the trade only.

THE BET, STATED PLAINLY. This clears the bar only if BD-rate falls faster than
speedup does. At threshold 16, A1 keeps ~1.70% speedup and needs its BD-rate
down 51% to reach ratio 25. At threshold 8 it keeps ~2.97% and needs 15%. If
BD-rate falls merely in proportion to speed at either setting, the ratio does
not move and the patch is no better than 0025. That is exactly the bet 0021
won and 06c lost, and I cannot tell from here which way it goes.

Stated that way threshold 8 looks like the easier bet. It is not, and the
correct comparison is worth spelling out. What the split needs is for the
blocks it spares to be OVER-REPRESENTED in BD-rate relative to the time they
carry. Writing f_s and f_b for the fractions of speedup and of BD-rate that
survive the split, the ratio improves iff f_s / f_b >= 25 / 23.3 = 1.07:

    threshold  8:  f_s = 0.91  ->  the 4xN blocks must carry >= 17% of the
                   BD-rate while carrying 9% of the time    (1.9x concentration)
    threshold 16:  f_s = 0.52  ->  the 4xN and 8xN blocks must carry >= 53% of
                   the BD-rate while carrying 48% of the time (1.1x)

Threshold 16 asks for far less concentration and is the better default. It is
also favoured by a second effect: the 91%/52% splits above were measured on a
192x128 clip, and 4K uses larger transform blocks, so at A1 resolutions the
small-block share of trellis time should be lower than 48% and threshold 16
should keep more speedup than it does here.

The default is therefore 16. Running 8 and 16 as two arms brackets the curve
and costs one extra rebuild rather than a patch edit:

    -DAVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM=8    keep trellis on 4x4 only
    -DAVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM=0    reproduces patch 0025 exactly
    -DAVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM=32   skip only on the largest blocks

Run at Speed 3. If both arms land below 25, the honest conclusion is that
dry-pass ranking genuinely needs trellised costs at every block size, and the
47% has to be attacked in the wet pass instead.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
index 593fd92..31d8d84 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
@@ -3012,7 +3012,60 @@ static AVM_INLINE void tx_type_rd(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x,
   av2_subtract_txb(x, AVM_PLANE_Y, plane_bsize, blk_col, blk_row, tx_size,
                    cpi->common.width, cpi->common.height, DCT_DCT);
   RD_STATS this_rd_stats;
-  const int skip_trellis = 0;
+  // Skip the trellis in the dry pass, but only on the transform blocks where
+  // it is expensive.
+  //
+  // BACKGROUND. A callgrind profile of this encoder puts trellis quantisation
+  // at ~47% of instructions retired, and av2/encoder/trellis_quant.c contains
+  // no speed-feature references at all. The two-pass dry pass declares that it
+  // avoids the trellis -- choose_tx_size_type_from_rd sets
+  // skip_trellis = x->apply_dry_pass_shortcuts ? 1 : 0 -- but this function,
+  // reached from try_tx_block_no_split once per transform block per transform
+  // partition candidate, had a hard-coded 0 and ran it at full strength.
+  //
+  // Patch 0025 skipped it here for every block and measured, at Speed 3:
+  //
+  //     A1  +3.26% / +0.14% BD   ratio 23.3   (bar 25)
+  //     A2  +4.23% / +0.17% BD   ratio 24.9   (bar 25)
+  //
+  // Both near-misses. A1 needs its BD-rate to fall only 7% at constant speed,
+  // and A2 needs 0.4%.
+  //
+  // WHY A SIZE SPLIT. Patch 0021 established the method: when a bundled change
+  // fails on ratio, split it and keep the members that are cheap in quality.
+  // Disabling only the uneven 4-way partitions kept 39% of the family's
+  // speedup for 6% of its BD-rate cost, giving ratio 207.7 where the whole
+  // family gave 30.6.
+  //
+  // The same split is available here, along transform block size. Trellis cost
+  // scales with the number of coefficients, so large transform blocks carry
+  // most of the time. But the ranking damage does not scale the same way: on a
+  // small block each coefficient is a larger share of the block's rate, so
+  // replacing the trellis with the cheap quantiser shifts the RD estimate
+  // proportionally more. Large blocks are therefore where skipping is cheap in
+  // quality and expensive in time -- exactly the members worth keeping.
+  //
+  // So the trellis is skipped only when the transform block's short side is at
+  // least AVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM. Below that the dry pass keeps the
+  // trellis and its ranking fidelity.
+  //
+  //     -DAVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM=0    reproduces patch 0025 exactly
+  //     -DAVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM=8    skip on 8x8 and larger
+  //     -DAVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM=32   skip only on the largest blocks
+  //
+  // Expected: less speedup than 0025, disproportionately less BD-rate. If the
+  // 16 threshold removes half the speedup and two thirds of the quality cost,
+  // A1 lands near 1.6% / 0.047% -- ratio 35. That is the shape of the bet, not
+  // a prediction of the numbers.
+#ifndef AVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM
+#define AVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM 16
+#endif
+  const int tx_short_side =
+      AVMMIN(tx_size_wide[tx_size], tx_size_high[tx_size]);
+  const int skip_trellis = (x->apply_dry_pass_shortcuts &&
+                            tx_short_side >= AVM_TX_DRY_TRELLIS_MIN_DIM)
+                               ? 1
+                               : 0;
   int dummy = 0;
   search_tx_type(cpi, x, 0, block, blk_row, blk_col, plane_bsize, tx_size,
                  &dummy, txb_ctx, ftxs_mode, skip_trellis, ref_rdcost,
